 180 million years  ago  [Music]  the breakup of this vast and resplendent  supercontinent saw the opening of the  atlantic ocean the migration of india  towards the asian subcontinent and the  utter isolation of australia and  antarctica  and so  we find our modern world  formed from the scattered fragments of  pangaea  but also of gondwana  panosia rodinia colombia and maybe even  kennel land and er  [Music]  each supercontinental age was distinct  in configuration duration climate and  character  pangaea was covered in great forests  while rodinia was barely greened at the  edges  panosha was a frozen wasteland while  colombia baked in endless deserts  yet despite their differences there are  certain conditions that all of these  supercontinents shared  and which life may face  in the coming supercontinental age  [Music]  alexander von humboldt was driven by an  insatiable curiosity for the world  around him  the german scholar traveled extensively  through the americas and russia  seeking to learn the secrets of those  often harsh but exotic lands  [Music]  wherever he traveled he erected weather  stations to measure the air temperature  pressure and rainfall  and he collected data whenever he could  to build a picture of weather variations  over time and space  [Music]  by 1830 he had collected enough data to  map out those conditions over a huge  swathe of western europe and russia  [Music]  von humboldt was the first to see  clearly in his data what any siberian  nomad could have told him  the further inland you travel the more  extreme the weather would become  winters in the interior were colder and  summers were hotter than in coastal  places at the same latitude  [Music]  this so-called continentality is a  feature of all large land masses on  earth today  and is more pronounced the larger the  continent  for instance antwerp close to the north  sea in northern belgium experiences an  average summer to winter temperature  range of nearly 15 degrees celsius  meanwhile saratov deep in central russia  sits at the same latitude but is some  2000 kilometers from the nearest ocean  its average temperature range is more  than double that of antwerp  [Music]  extreme conditions arise from the  differences in how land and sea absorb  the heat from the sun  in summer land areas heat up much more  quickly than the ocean which can  distribute its heat at depth  whereas in winter the oceans still have  some store of heat left over to create  milder conditions while the land cools  much more quickly  coastal regions reap the benefits of the  ocean  and the continental interiors are left  out in the cold  or the heat  [Music]  and it is not only temperature that  defines continentality  any satellite image of earth today will  reveal that the deep hearts of  continents are often deprived of the  green of life  they are deserts  where little rain falls and living  things struggle to survive  one reason for this is the sheer  distance rain is ultimately derived from  warm oceans and the greater the distance  from those oceans the less rain that is  left to fall  but the clouds and life-giving rain are  also blocked by mountain chains  the warm moist air is forced up steep  mountain slopes where it cools condenses  and rains out  the resulting rain shadow mirrors the  real shadow of the mountain but can  extend much further inland  [Music]  if such extreme conditions are to be  found inside the modest continental land  masses on earth today then we can only  begin to imagine the  supercontinentality that held sway in  the interiors of pangaea  or rodinia  or  [Music]  supercontinents positioned at low  latitudes close to the equator would see  their central regions transformed into  baked dusty deserts where not a drop of  rain would fall for centuries  or even  millennia  [Music]  even for the likes of panoshia  straddling the south pole ice caps would  be slow to grow if the snow that fed  them never arrived  the suturing of crustal fragments during  the assembly of supercontinents also  generated mountains in staggering  abundance  with every continental collision huge  ranges and plateaus to rival the  himalayas and tibet are heaved out of  the tortured ground  monumental towering belts stop weather  in its tracks creating rain shadows that  bring the desert ever closer to the  coasts  with vast mountain ranges creating a  high altitude labyrinth of blockages and  diversions wind and weather is whipped  into a frenzy  high level plateaus pump warmth and  moisture into the upper atmosphere  driving monsoons that sweep their  devastation across the coastal  landscapes  with terrible regularity  [Music]  flash floods and ephemeral rivers from  these violent storms create huge inland  deltas that irrigate the arid lowlands  for a time  but these same winds  now stripped of their moisture  howl across the featureless deserts  sending dust storms tumbling viciously  for thousands of kilometers  [Music]  such harsh conditions in the centers of  most of earth's supercontinents would  have been little concern for life  since living things only began to  colonize the land surfaces of gondwana  and pangaea  but for those pioneering forests insects  and teetering tetrapods  supercontinental life was unforgiving  many places were all but uninhabitable  the dry interior deserts  the wild wind-whipped mountain tops  but towards the coasts and along  sustaining rivers and deltas the  supercontinents offered a bounty of  newly exposed mineral nutrients and all  living things thrived  populations on land boomed and crashed  with the march of the seasons  compared to the constancy of life  beneath the waves  [Music]  but there was plenty of time to adapt  the dance of the continents may seem  frantic when we fast forward through  earth's history  but the reign of pangaea spanned the  entire ancestry of the dinosaurs  from the first slithering steps onto  land in the devonian to the mighty  long-necked lizards  of the jurassic  [Music]  we cannot truly know what it is to live  on a supercontinent  since that is not something humans have  ever experienced even in genetic memory  and unless humankind persists for  another 250 million years or so we may  never know  we are living through a time of  continental dispersion  but another supercontinental age  is on the horizon  even now the assembly is beginning  the current movements of the plates will  bring continents together one by one  africa and arabia will grind northwards  into europe closing the mediterranean  and creating a mountain range in its  place  australia and antarctica will collide at  high latitudes and may soon creep north  together  eventually the atlantic will stop  widening and its crust will begin to be  subducted  [Music]  north and south america will rotate  towards each other as they bear down on  the afro-eurasian amalgamation  and as the north atlantic disappears the  indo-atlantic is  trapped there's a huge inland sea  between south america  india and southeast asia  when the collisions are finally  concluded  250 million years from now  perhaps all of the modern landmass will  be combined into one immense  supercontinent  pangaea  ultima  [Music]  like the generations of supercontinents  before it pangea ultima will be built  from the shattered remains of its  ancestors  but will have a character all of its own  for a biosphere like ours now so  thoroughly wedded to our continental  land  it is hard to say whether such unity  will be a blessing  or a curse  [Music]  you've been watching the entire history  of the earth  don't forget to like and subscribe and  leave a comment to let us know what you  think  thanks for watching  and we'll see you next time  [Music]  you


